{
  "dom_id": "step-q216062",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 & |\\times 3 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5s-2t=-3 \\\\ 4s+t=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4s+t & =8 \\\\ t & =8-4s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $t=8-4s$ into the first equation and then solve for $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s-2t & =-3 \\\\ 5s-2(8-4s) & =-3 \\\\ 5s-16+8s & =-3 \\\\ 13s & =13 \\\\ s & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $t,$ we substitute $s=1$ into the equation $t=8-4s$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =8-4s \\\\  & =8-4(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $s=1$ and $t=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
